Created attachment 4056
transiently lock environment during as.list.environment and eapply

The issue ultimately comes down to the fact that `env2list` (the c function `as.list` calls, precomputes the size of the list needed (ie how many symbols are bound in `e`) before evaluating those symbols (and thus triggering the active binding), thus as is walks the environment in this case, populating the list, it will reach the number of elements it thought it would have - and thus preallocated the list for - ie that were there before the active binding fired, and then find another. When it attempts to add that new one to the result list, it gets an index out of bounds error.

In particular, for the non-sorted (default) case, `ans` (the SEXP returned) is allocated via these lines:

```
// k := length(env) = envxlength(env) :
    if (env == R_BaseEnv || env == R_BaseNamespace)
	k = BuiltinSize(all, 0);
    else if (HASHTAB(env) != R_NilValue)
	k = HashTableSize(HASHTAB(env), all);
    else
	k = FrameSize(FRAME(env), all);

    PROTECT(names = allocVector(STRSXP, k));
    PROTECT(ans = allocVector(VECSXP, k));
```
(3358-3367)

`FrameSize`/`HashTableSize` do *not* fire active bindings, so b is not created, so k represents the pre-binding-firing size (1 for our replication code). 

`k` is not then used for a `for` loop, but rather reinitialized and used as a count in a `while` loop (it would be twice, but the first one is the one that fails).

first, when retrieving the values, here:

```c
   k = 0;
    if (env == R_BaseEnv || env == R_BaseNamespace)
	BuiltinValues(all, 0, ans, &k);
    else if (HASHTAB(env) != R_NilValue)
	HashTableValues(HASHTAB(env), all, ans, &k);
    else
	FrameValues(FRAME(env), all, ans, &k);
```
3369-3375

FrameValues is defined like so:
```c
static void FrameValues(SEXP frame, int all, SEXP values, int *indx)
{
    if (all) {
	while (frame != R_NilValue) {
#         define DO_FrameValues						\
	    SEXP value = BINDING_VALUE(frame);				\
	    if (TYPEOF(value) == PROMSXP) {				\
		PROTECT(value);						\
		value = eval(value, R_GlobalEnv);			\
		UNPROTECT(1);						\
	    }								\
	    SET_VECTOR_ELT(values, *indx, lazy_duplicate(value));	\
	    (*indx)++

	    DO_FrameValues;
	    frame = CDR(frame);
	}
    } else {
	while (frame != R_NilValue) {
	    if (NONEMPTY_(frame)) {
		DO_FrameValues;
	    }
	    frame = CDR(frame);
	}
    }
}

```

(`HashTableValues` simply calls `FrameValues` on chunks of the hash table). 

Notice that this is a *while* loop which contains *`BINDING_VALUE(frame)`*, but it is populating *`values`* (`ans`) which, recall is of length pre-active-binding k. 

Thus when BINDING_VALUE(frame) grabs `a`, and fires the binding, it assigns into the environment currently being walked, then returns the value which is placed in `values` and `indx` is incremented **to the pre-computed value of k, which is out of bounds given 0-based indexing**, then the while loop checks if there are more bound values ***finds that there (now) are*** and continues. When it now finds the value of b and attempts to put it into `values`, its attempting to write out of bounds, thus the error. (though it does seem like the error message has an off by one error, really its attempting to write to element 2/1, or position 1/0).

Allowing for this type of situation to succeed would be possible (e.g., by detecting if `indx` inside `FrameValues` gets too big and resizing `values` if it does), but I'm not convinced it would be desirable. This really seems like something you should not be doing. Note the case where `b` already exists and would just have its value *changed* already works, so active bindings that hold onto and increment a counter would already work even in this case wehre the counter is placed in the same environment as the binding (already a pretty strange thing to do i think).

Instead the attached patch transiently locks and then unlocks (if it was previously unlocked) the environment passed to `as.list.environment` and `eapply` down in C code. Tests included.

After the patch you get a (somewhat) nicer error message:

```
> e <- new.env()
> makeActiveBinding("a", function() { assign("b", 1, envir = e); 1 }, e)
> x <- as.list(e)
Error in assign("b", 1, envir = e) : 
  cannot add bindings to a locked environment
```

Documentation has been updated to indicate the transient locking that informs the new error message.
